    <text class="diagram-note" x="16" y="1504" font-size="15">Drawn with both changes; either one</text>
    <text class="diagram-note" x="16" y="1526" font-size="15">alone also narrows the gap.</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    <p class="kicker">Figure</p>
    <p>
      Coronary perfusion pressure is the gap between relaxation-phase aortic pressure and right atrial
      pressure. When right atrial pressure is higher and relaxation-phase aortic pressure is unchanged, the
      gap is smaller. When compressions pause, aortic pressure falls toward right atrial
      pressure and the gap narrows quickly; the longer the pause, the closer it comes to closing. When compressions continue but are ineffective, a lower relaxation-phase
      aortic pressure, a higher right atrial pressure, or both, can leave a gap narrower than the
      open gradient; how narrow depends on how ineffective they are. Flow depends on that pressure difference divided by
      resistance. A narrower difference, or a higher resistance, means less flow. Neither pressure
      alone sets coronary perfusion.
    </p>
  </figcaption>
</figure>
